  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nA weak surface boundary sits across the forecast area today. Winds \nare light and variable area-wide. Temperatures are fairly uniform \nand still seasonably warm, but dew points are lower across far \nnorthern KS, north of the boundary. Water vapor imagery and H5 \nanalysis show a small cyclonic circulation over northern TX and a \ntrough axis over the Northern High Plains, which should quickly \ntraverse the Midwest bringing another, more obvious, cold front \nthrough the area late tonight/early Wednesday. Winds will increase \nout of the northwest behind the front and could gust 20 to 30 mph \ntomorrow. Above average temperatures tomorrow will transition to \nmuch cooler conditions for Thursday and especially by Friday when a \nsurface ridge builds in from the north. Low temps both Friday \nand Saturday morning will be near freezing. Highs Friday will \nstruggle to reach 50 degrees. A slow warming trend commences this\nweekend.\n\nPrecipitation chances also return this weekend when our next system \napproaches Saturday night and Sunday. Long range models show a cut-\noff low pivoting into the Southern Plains on Sunday. There are \nslight timing differences, but models indicate low level moisture \nincreasing ahead of the system as the sfc low dives southward into \ncentral portions of TX. A large sfc ridge over the Upper Midwest \ncould keep drier conditions in eastern KS so uncertainty remains on \nhow far north rain will develop. NBM currently has 20 to 30 percent \nchances across the area so will maintain those low chances for now \nand will monitor model trends into midweek.",
